<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=radioactivity">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radioactivity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Radioactive decay, Nuclear decay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radioactivity, or radioactive decay, is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation, discovered in 1896 by Henri Becquerel when uranium salts blackened a photographic plate wrapped in black paper. Marie Curie showed that the rays were a property of atoms; with Pierre Curie she coined the term &quot;radioactivity&quot; and isolated polonium and radium. Ernest Rutherford and Frederick Soddy were the first to realise that many decay processes turn one element into another. Decay is used to estimate the ages of rocks and organic matter.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radioactivity.jpg" alt="Radioactivity" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Becquerel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Becquerel_plate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
